    <section class="danr">
     <h1>Emergency First Aid for Amputation, Unconsciousness, and Severe Bleeding</h1>
     <div class=en1><script language='javascript' src='/ad/h.js'></script></div>  
      <p>In a trauma situation with life-threatening injuries, follow this prioritized sequence:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Ensure scene safety.</strong> Do not become a second victim. Assess hazards (traffic, fire, structural collapse).</li>
<li><strong>Call emergency services (911 in US) immediately.</strong> State location, number of victims, and nature of injuries clearly.</li>
<li><strong>Address the "ABCs" in order:</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Airway:</strong> Open with jaw-thrust (if spinal injury suspected) or head-tilt/chin-lift. Clear visible obstructions.</li>
<li><strong>Breathing:</strong> Look, listen, feel for ≤10 seconds. If absent or agonal, begin CPR (30 compressions : 2 breaths) if trained.</li>
<li><strong>Circulation/Severe bleeding:</strong> Apply direct, firm pressure with a clean cloth or gauze. Pack deep wounds. Use a commercial tourniquet <em>only</em> if direct pressure fails on a limb bleed; note application time.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Manage amputation:</strong> Wrap the severed part in moist sterile gauze, place in a sealed plastic bag, and put the bag in a container of ice water (<em>do not</em> place part directly on ice). Transport with the patient.</li>
<li><strong>Monitor and reassure.</strong> Keep the person warm, calm, and still. Do not give food/drink.</li>
<li><strong>Handoff to EMS.</strong> Provide concise report: mechanism, injuries, interventions, vital signs if known.</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>Critical reminders:</strong> Control massive hemorrhage before CPR if both are present. Never remove an impaled object. Tourniquets are safe when applied correctly for ≤2 hours; modern guidelines support early use for life-threatening limb bleeding.</p>
